{"v": 1, "ausgabe": 10, "datum": "2026-11-23", "titel": "", "erstellt": "2026-11-23T17:55:00+01:00", "abgeglichen": "2026-11-23T18:26:06+01:00", "wetter": "Salzburg 0 to 3 °C, light rain, 95 % chance of showers", "sonne": "Sunrise 7:24", "kurz": ["Consulting Deep Dive, part 8: commercial due diligence, the fastest lane from strategy work into private equity", "Kellogg's new MBA class: 528 students, a median GMAT Focus of 685 and a record share of STEM graduates", "An Anthropic mathematician says he has proved the Riemann hypothesis with Claude"], "anzahlMeldungen": 4, "ressorts": [{"name": "Consulting", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Consulting Deep Dive, part 8: commercial due diligence, the fastest lane from strategy work into private equity", "text": "Commercial due diligence (CDD) is the work a buyer commissions before signing a deal: is the market as large and as fast-growing as the seller claims, are the customers loyal, can the target defend its margins, and does the investment thesis survive contact with reality? For private equity funds, which buy companies largely with borrowed money and must sell them again within a few years, that answer is worth paying for under extreme time pressure. A whole tier of the consulting industry has organised itself around it.\n\nBain is the reference point. Its private equity practice says it has run more than 22,000 due diligence cases since 2000 and advised on over half of all scale buyout transactions in that period, combining strategic, commercial, operational and technology questions. L.E.K. Consulting presents CDD as a core service alongside vendor and operational due diligence and portfolio value creation, and says it works on more than 400 projects a year for private equity clients; one company page puts its total at roughly 4,000 transactions over the past decade. OC&C, a boutique strong in consumer, retail and business services, is described as a diligence specialist with outsized deal flow; one of its partners has said that about half of the firm's work is for private equity, from pre-deal diligence to value creation after the purchase. EY-Parthenon, Strategy& Deals and smaller houses such as CIL also live largely off buy-side and sell-side diligence, while Roland Berger is regarded as the European heavyweight for industrial and automotive deals.\n\nWhat does the work feel like? Expect market modelling, a great deal of primary research (customer interviews, surveys, expert calls) and relentless pressure-testing of the investment thesis, usually on short, intense projects whose deliverable is a view the fund can defend before its investment committee. At EY-Parthenon, consultants typically start as generalists and only later align with industries, which means exposure to many sectors in quick succession.\n\nThe exit logic is straightforward. Private equity is the most common exit from L.E.K., and the one its work is built for. Two years of CDD teach you to think like an investor: which markets deserve a premium multiple, how funds frame value creation plans, and you meet deal teams across the table. The gap, as career guides point out, is technical: CDD firms build commercial cases, not leveraged buyout models, so candidates usually have to teach themselves LBO mechanics before recruiting for associate roles at funds. Corporate development and M&A teams inside companies, and corporate strategy, are the other frequent destinations. Industry voices also note that the market has become harder: with exits from fund portfolios slower, more consulting work has shifted from pure pre-deal diligence towards value creation in companies funds already own.\n\nThe trade-off against generalist strategy work is breadth. CDD-heavy firms give you deep investor exposure early, but fewer long transformation projects inside large corporates; MBB, and Bain in particular, offer both but are harder to get into. A sensible sequence for a would-be investor is therefore Bain or a CDD specialist for two to three years, deliberate exposure to private equity cases, then a move to a fund or to corporate development.", "url": "https://www.bain.com/industry-expertise/private-equity/", "quelle": "Bain, Private Equity", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@6c412a6f25f2722179298f618b5d69cf2a26b69e/cdc3f14c71279c37.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Business Schools and Master's", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Kellogg's new MBA class: 528 students, a median GMAT Focus of 685 and a record share of STEM graduates", "text": "Northwestern University's Kellogg School of Management has published the profile of its Two-Year MBA Class of 2028, and it says a good deal about where elite business education is heading. The school enrolled 528 students this autumn. A record 45 per cent majored in science, technology, engineering or mathematics (some students count twice because of double majors). The median undergraduate GPA among graduates of US institutions is 3.72 on a 4.0 scale.\n\nThe headline number for anyone preparing applications is the test score: the class has a median GMAT Focus score of 685. Poets and Quants, which analysed the profile, reports that the international share fell to 35 per cent; Kellogg's Class of 2026 was still 40 per cent international. Poets and Quants also highlights a more generous GI Bill benefit for military veterans, a group American schools court actively.\n\nBoth trends are long-running. Business schools increasingly recruit engineers and quantitative graduates, whom employers in consulting and technology prize, and STEM-designated programmes in the US give international graduates longer post-study work permission. The falling international share, by contrast, suggests that foreign candidates have become more cautious about American programmes.\n\nFor European pre-experience programmes such as the Master in Management, the read-across is indirect: if fewer internationals head to American schools, more of that talent may stay in Europe, which could stiffen competition at HEC, LBS or INSEAD in coming cycles.", "url": "https://poetsandquants.com/2026/11/22/kelloggs-mba-class-of-2028-more-stem-fewer-internationals-and-a-bigger-gi-bill-benefit-for-veterans/?pq-category=class-profiles-demographics", "quelle": "Poets and Quants", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@6c412a6f25f2722179298f618b5d69cf2a26b69e/02d93097fb649de9.mp3"}]}, {"name": "AI and Claude", "meldungen": [{"titel": "An Anthropic mathematician says he has proved the Riemann hypothesis with Claude", "text": "Levent Alpöge, a mathematician at Anthropic, claims to have proved the Riemann hypothesis, arguably the most celebrated open problem in mathematics, with Claude as his working partner. First posed in 1859, the conjecture concerns where the zeros of a particular function lie and, through them, how the prime numbers are distributed. It is one of the seven Millennium Prize problems set by the Clay Mathematics Institute, each carrying a bounty of one million dollars, and in a quarter of a century only one of them, the Poincaré conjecture, has officially been solved.\n\nAlpöge announced the result in a statement on his personal website, not through Anthropic, which has so far neither commented nor involved itself. \"I believe I have, working with Claude, discovered a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis,\" he writes, adding that the method extends to the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis and, he expects, eventually to the Extended version too. He says he and Claude have also formalised the argument in Lean, a proof-checking language that lets a computer verify every logical step. He texted Anthropic's chief executive, Dario Amodei, late on Sunday night and appears to have delayed publication until after the company's stock market listing, due on Thursday. In August Anthropic itself had said it was working on the problem with Claude, while cautioning that it did not expect those techniques to lead to a proof.\n\nThe claim is, for now, exactly that. It has not been independently verified, and the Clay Institute's rules require a solution to be published and to withstand at least two years of scrutiny, earning \"general acceptance\" among mathematicians, before any prize is considered. Even so, the announcement lands amid an extraordinary run for AI in mathematics. In September OpenAI said it had cracked the Navier-Stokes problem on fluid flow, deploying 10,000 agents and spending millions of dollars; that result is widely regarded as credible but has been clouded by allegations that it drew on unpublished work shared with its tools, which OpenAI denies. Alpöge had himself been working on Navier-Stokes with the NYU mathematician Tristan Buckmaster, and in July he said he had settled the Jacobian conjecture with Claude.\n\nWhat comes next is slow, unglamorous checking: mathematicians and proof assistants will pick through the Lean formalisation, and the field is already struggling to absorb the volume of AI-assisted results. If the proof survives, it would rank among the most significant scientific achievements credited to an AI system, and it would arrive, conveniently, in the very week Anthropic goes public.", "url": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1010051/anthropic-riemann-hypothesis-millennium-prize-problem", "quelle": "The Verge", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@6c412a6f25f2722179298f618b5d69cf2a26b69e/8a30f105fb7255ae.mp3"}]}, {"name": "Policy Check", "meldungen": [{"titel": "Brussels' budget broker trims the next EU budget and revives a tax on Big Tech", "text": "The decision. European Council President António Costa has tabled a revised negotiating box for the EU's next seven-year budget, the multiannual financial framework (MFF) for 2028 to 2034, ahead of a special summit on 26 and 27 November. According to several reports over the weekend, it would cut about 180.5 billion euros from the Commission's original plan and add a 3 per cent digital services tax on large technology companies as a new revenue source for the EU. Any such tax requires the unanimous consent of all 27 member states; if the November summit fails, the next opportunity is the European Council on 17 and 18 December.\n\nThe case made. Costa's argument, carried to capitals on his tour before the summit, is that there is no deal without new EU-level revenue (\"own resources\"), because otherwise every euro of new spending on defence or competitiveness must come from national contributions. Critics attack from two sides: net payers want a smaller budget, while a tax aimed mostly at American firms risks retaliation from Washington. The Commission itself dropped a digital levy from its July 2025 proposal, and France is reported to have rejected the compromise. Update, Monday morning: according to Der Standard, the revised plan is meeting broad resistance from governments, the European Parliament, farmers and regions alike, so the dispute runs on into the summit.\n\nThe numbers. The Commission's July 2025 proposal amounted to 1,984 billion euros in current prices, or 1.26 per cent of EU gross national income (GNI), including 0.11 per cent for repaying NextGenerationEU debt (European Parliament Research Service, EPRS). Excluding that repayment it equals 1.15 per cent of GNI, against 1.13 per cent when the current 2021 to 2027 budget was agreed; inflation has since eroded the current framework to about 1.02 per cent (EPRS, 2026). In 2025 prices the Commission asked for 1,763 billion euros in commitments; the Irish presidency's October draft cut that to 1,622 billion, about 141 billion or 8 per cent less. The Commission's five proposed new own resources were to raise 58.5 billion euros a year, and a European Parliament package of digital, gambling and crypto taxes was estimated at up to 25 billion euros a year.\n\nReality check. The claim that the EU budget leans ever more heavily on national GNI contributions is supported by the Commission's own figures and EPRS analyses. The reported cut points the same way as the Irish draft, only further. What remains unsupported is any published estimate of what a 3 per cent digital tax would actually yield, how it would interact with national digital taxes already in force, or how large the risk of US retaliation is. Whether the EU should spend more or less is a value judgement the data cannot settle, and the exact baseline of the 180.5 billion figure (current or constant prices) could not be verified.\n\nEvidence rating: Thinly grounded. The underlying funding gap is well documented, but the specific remedy, a 3 per cent levy on Big Tech, has been put forward without a public estimate of its revenue or its economic cost.", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMic0FVX3lxTE1yaXIwQkV6cElINVUxaC15YXI1WVVIS3FQZlpmV2dSdGVQS2pTeW84blJuZ0VfMnMta1p4dW1HUnQtbU1QRVlsZXNzTjVPSVBabTlMT3J2cURFUjBKQk5JZEZ2MU1GVUhnVVNGNmpJdkV2RlU?oc=5", "quelle": "CEO Magazyn", "audio": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@6c412a6f25f2722179298f618b5d69cf2a26b69e/376c8efceb65a406.mp3"}]}], "fuss": "Created on 23 November 2026 at 07:30 by Claude. Last updated 17:55. Sources linked under each report. Gmail could not be checked; the connector needs a fresh sign-in. Outlook brought one new reply this morning (Deutsche Rentenversicherung), nothing further by 17:55.", "privat": {"iv": "gmJgfCGTifexuEzxJBvJCA==", "ct": "", "mac": "JTHRVHVJ3cgUpzjQ/YetHYDPP1+KDrfPIPPASgEnr50="}, "audioGesamt": {"url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/GregorW05/apps@6c412a6f25f2722179298f618b5d69cf2a26b69e/g14ef7e26e10481f.mp3", "kapitel": [0.0, 234.744, 358.728, 538.344], "dauer": 771.648}}